// What people are saying: a snapshot of Slack threads and Jira tickets that bear on CVR, from the last 48 hours only (Fri Oct 30, about 2:40 PM ET to Sun Nov 1, about 2:45 PM ET). Read Nov 1 ~2:45 PM ET: no new posts since 9 AM in #released, #ask-deap-bot-squad, #peak_daily_performance_standup or #promotions; workspace-wide only automated alerts. Added the Oct 30 4:35 to 6:23 PM Bot Squad thread on blocking versus filtering the Redbubble botnet (thread replies missed by earlier runs). The #released quantity-discount post (Oct 30 1:08 PM) and the #promotions November sale post (Oct 30 1:36 PM) aged out of the window.
const SLACK = [
  { icon: '⚖️', site: 'RB', title: 'Redbubble botnet stays filtered out of the numbers rather than blocked, for now', when: 'Oct 30', url: 'https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C095R5DPN3H/p1793392541110199?thread_ts=1793369358.698449&cid=C095R5DPN3H', refs: [{ go: 'rb|overall', l: 'Redbubble CVR' }, { jira: 'DEAP-6089' }], status: 'Decision open', tone: 'warn', channel: '#ask-deap-bot-squad', fx: 'No, for this page: the Redbubble numbers here already leave out this botnet\'s browser signature. The daily bot check does not, so its Redbubble bot share reads higher than what is shown here.', body: 'Investigating a Redbubble bot alert, the Bot Squad found the rise in traffic scoring 30 to 69 is the known desktop-browser, phone-size-screen botnet, not a new one. It is filtered out of analytics instead of blocked, because Cloudflare cannot target screen size and the risk of blocking shoppers is high. A block rule is ready but on hold: it would challenge about 5 orders a day, in exchange for about 5.2M fewer requests and about 1.43M fewer page loads in analytics a week. Most people would never see the challenge, and the rest can solve it and keep shopping. TeePublic does not yet capture the browser signature in Amplitude that Redbubble uses; adding it is going into the current sprint.', tldr: 'The Bot Squad is weighing a block on the Redbubble desktop botnet that would cost about 5 orders a day; for now it stays filtered out of the numbers.', pts: ['The rise in Redbubble traffic scoring 30 to 69 is the known desktop botnet (DEAP-6089), not a new one.', 'A block rule is ready but on hold: about 5 orders a day challenged vs about 5.2M fewer requests a week.', 'TeePublic will start capturing the same browser signature in Amplitude (current sprint).'], qs: ['How many orders is it worth risking for cleaner numbers and lower server load?'], why: 'If the block ships, watch Redbubble orders for a small dip; the CVR shown here should barely move since the botnet is already left out.' },
  { icon: '🛒', site: 'TP', title: 'TeePublic sticker cross-sell is live; early read is negative but small', when: 'Oct 30', url: 'https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0C9X6MHV89', refs: [{ go: 'tp|overall', l: 'TeePublic CVR' }, { go: 'tp|revenue', l: 'TeePublic revenue' }], status: 'Too early', tone: 'warn', channel: '#peak_daily_performance_standup', fx: 'No: with about 1,700 engagements it is too early to call, and it does not move the numbers shown here yet.', body: 'The sticker cross-sell launched on Oct 29. Its first data looks negative, but it only has about 1,700 engagements so far and needs more time before anyone reads it. The style selection pills test also had its order value metric corrected: the earlier figure divided revenue by all exposed visitors rather than by orders, so it was swapped for true order value and the old one archived. That test needs a few more days, and desktop is doing better than mobile, so different experiences may follow.', tldr: 'The sticker cross-sell reads negative on about 1,700 engagements, too few to judge; the style pills test needs a few more days.', pts: ['Sticker cross-sell launched Oct 29; the first data is negative on about 1,700 engagements.', 'Style pills test: order value is now measured as revenue divided by orders, and it needs a few more days.', 'Desktop is outperforming mobile on the style pills test, so separate experiences may follow.'], qs: ['How many engagements are enough to call the sticker cross-sell?'], why: 'Do not read the cross-sell as a loss yet; check the A/B tests page in a few days.' },
  { icon: '🤖', site: 'RB', title: 'One machine in Belgium sent about 163K product clicks to Redbubble in a day', when: 'Oct 30', url: 'https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C095R5DPN3H/p1793383023930509?thread_ts=1793289980.123849&cid=C095R5DPN3H', refs: [{ go: 'rb|overall', l: 'Redbubble CVR' }, { jira: 'DEAP-6942' }], status: 'Fix drafted', tone: 'warn', channel: '#ask-deap-bot-squad', fx: 'Yes, for Redbubble: it behaved like a browser, so Cloudflare scored it as human and it counted in product clicks and pageviews with no add to carts or orders, which pulls CVR down on Oct 27 to 29.', body: 'The Bot Squad traced the "Belgian bot traffic" on Redbubble product pages to a single machine: one Orange Belgium address running Chrome with one fixed browser signature. On Oct 28 it made 99.6% of all Belgian product clicks (about 163K vs about 700 on a normal day) with zero add to carts and zero orders. At the edge it sent about 1.07M requests from Oct 27 to 29, mostly to the search API, and it stopped around 3 PM ET Oct 29. Nothing blocked it because it looked like a real browser and the search API has no rate limit. A rate limit for that endpoint is drafted, starting in log-only mode, and the team wants TeePublic to get the same bot-identifying check Redbubble already has before peak.', tldr: 'A single Belgian machine made about 163K Redbubble product clicks on Oct 28 and no purchases; a rate limit is drafted.', pts: ['One address and one browser signature made 99.6% of Belgian product clicks on Oct 28.', 'About 1.07M edge requests Oct 27 to 29; it stopped around 3 PM ET Oct 29.', 'A log-only rate limit on the search API is drafted (DEAP-6942); TeePublic parity check before peak.'], qs: ['Should Redbubble CVR on Oct 27 to 29 be read with this traffic set aside?'], why: 'A single scraper can move a whole day of Redbubble CVR; expect similar days to read low until the rate limit is enforced.' },
  { icon: '⚾', site: 'TP', title: 'Brewers World Series win lifts TeePublic, softness expected as it cools', when: 'Oct 30', url: 'https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0C9X6MHV89', refs: [{ go: 'tp|revenue', l: 'TeePublic revenue' }, { go: 'tp|overall', l: 'TeePublic CVR' }], status: 'Cooling', tone: 'good', channel: '#peak_daily_performance_standup', fx: 'Yes: Brewers designs were about 9% of TeePublic revenue on the day they won and about 21% the next day, which lifts orders and revenue; a pull-back is expected Oct 30 and 31.', body: 'Marketing saw a late-night surge in shopping spend on Oct 28 that tracked the Brewers World Series win, and TeePublic revenue grew on Oct 29 because of it. Paid spend got a bump to follow the demand ($5K in one hour) with revenue following the spend. The expectation now is some softness today and tomorrow as Brewers content falls back. For comparison, last year\'s World Series win added only about 3% of revenue the following day, so this is not a trend to count on for next year.', tldr: 'Brewers designs were about 9% of TeePublic revenue on win day and 21% the next, and are now falling back.', pts: ['Brewers content: about 9% of revenue on the day they won, about 21% the next day.', 'Paid spend was raised for an hour to follow the demand; revenue followed.', 'Expect some softness Oct 30 and 31 as it fades.'], qs: ['How much of the Oct 29 CVR lift is Brewers designs vs the rest of the site?'], why: 'Read Oct 29 to 31 TeePublic numbers against non-event days; the Brewers spike inflates the high and then the comparison.' },
];

